-- ============================================================================
-- PD6 EXPERT DEEP SEED: Flow Assurance, advanced tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE BOUNDARY AND THE CHEMICAL. Both of its findings are
-- invisible from the Professional answers, and both are only reachable by
-- comparing two engine returns against each other, which is what an expert
-- tier is for.
--
-- THE JOULE-THOMSON TERM IS APPLIED UNDAMPED. `steadyStateProfile` carries the
-- JT drop LINEARLY along the line, jtCoeff x dp x x/L, and subtracts it from
-- an exponential that is not linear. Solve the same balance with a constant JT
-- sink per foot, which is exactly what a linear pressure profile implies, and
-- the JT offset at the outlet is s Lc (1 - exp(-ntu)) rather than s L. The
-- correct weighting is therefore (1 - exp(-ntu))/ntu, and on the capstone line
-- that is 0.46766: the engine applies 2.1383 times the term it should. In degF
-- it is the difference between 10.6875 and 4.9981, and the arrival moves from
-- 72.9257 to 67.2364 degF against a 71.4 degF hydrate boundary. THE VERDICT
-- FLIPS. Comfortably outside becomes comfortably inside, and a project reads
-- continuous methanol from day one out of a factor of two.
--
-- AND THE TIER MUST NOT OVERSTATE IT, WHICH IS THE HARDER HALF. Push the same
-- line to three times its length and the engine reports an arrival 9.578 degF
-- BELOW the seabed it is losing heat to, while `uForArrivalTemp` in the same
-- file refuses that exact temperature as an impossible target. That reads like
-- a proof of absurdity and it is not one. A Joule-Thomson term is a REAL heat
-- sink: gas expanding down a line does work, and a line can genuinely arrive
-- below its ambient. The correctly damped reading on that same three-times
-- line is 38.313 degF, which is also below the 39.2 degF seabed, by 0.887
-- degF. So the sign is not the defect. THE SIZE IS: the engine's excursion
-- below ambient is 10.8 times the correct one. A lesson that argued "below
-- ambient is impossible, therefore the term is wrong" would be right about the
-- term for a reason that is false, and would teach a learner to reject a
-- correct answer the next time one arrives.
--
-- A FAMILY OF NaN-IS-FALSY FAILS-OPEN, AND THEY ARE NOT ALL THE SAME SIZE.
--
--   `overallU` CATCHES A NaN BURIAL RESISTANCE AND DROPS THE TERM. The guard
--   is `if (Number.isFinite(r)) resistances.push(...)`, so a burial depth
--   shallower than half the coated diameter, which is what a depth entered to
--   the pipe TOP rather than the centreline looks like on a big weight coat,
--   returns a trenched line silently reclassified as an exposed one: U 0.9452
--   against 0.5428, 74.13 percent high, with ok TRUE and no note. A term that
--   cannot be computed is not a term worth zero. It is the tier's cleanest
--   fails-open and it is still the WEAKER of the two, because the return
--   carries a `resistances` array and a caller who counts it gets five entries
--   where six were expected.
--
--   `cooldownTime` TURNS A NaN MASS INTO A ZERO MASS, AND ONE BAD SLOT FAILS
--   OPEN WHERE TWO REFUSE CORRECTLY. Both slots read as
--   `(contents?.massLbPerFt || 0)`, NaN is falsy, and the only guard is
--   `!(mcp > 0)` on the TOTAL. Both slots bad refuses properly. ONE slot bad
--   returns ok true, a no-touch time, a time constant and a full 33 station
--   table, with no note and no error, short by exactly the dropped slot's
--   share of M Cp: on the capstone line a NaN shell mass returns 1.9546 hr
--   against 2.5446, 23.19 percent low, on a number a platform is evacuated
--   against. THIS IS THE SHARPER OF THE TWO, and the reason is structural
--   rather than numerical: `overallU` returns something a caller can COUNT,
--   and `cooldownTime` returns `ok, hours, timeConstantHr, stations` and
--   nothing else, so the caller cannot detect the loss from the return at any
--   effort. The module's own discipline, "A NaN propagates into a refusal; a
--   plausible wrong number does not", is defeated two calls later, because
--   `pipeMassLbPerFt` and `contentsMassLbPerFt` both return a BARE NaN rather
--   than a refusal object and the NaN then meets a `|| 0`.
--
--   AND `inhibitionRequirement` WITH NO SUBCOOLING AT ALL ANSWERS "NO
--   INHIBITOR IS NEEDED". The guard is `!(need > 0)` and `!(NaN > 0)` is true,
--   so the branch written for a fluid already outside the hydrate region also
--   catches a caller who supplied nothing. It returns ok true, required false,
--   and a note that prints the literal string "NaN F" to a user. The other two
--   return a wrong number. This one returns a DECISION NOT TO INHIBIT A LINE.
--
-- SIZED ONE WAY, CHECKED ANOTHER, AND THE TWO ARE NEVER COMPARED.
-- `inhibitionRequirement` sizes the dose with the Hammerschmidt INVERSE and
-- then checks it by calling `depression`, which above 25 weight percent
-- reports NIELSEN-BUCKLIN. On the capstone requirement of 45.8 degF it returns
-- 38.5919 weight percent methanol with ok TRUE, and its own
-- `depressionCheck.nielsenBucklinF`, in the same returned object, says that
-- concentration delivers 39.2154 degF. That is 6.5846 degF short of what was
-- asked for and 1.1846 degF short of the BARE subcooling before any margin, so
-- the design the engine passes leaves the line inside the hydrate region.
-- Nothing in the function compares the two numbers. The honest Nielsen-Bucklin
-- dose is 42.9843 weight percent, 4.3923 points higher and 19.96 percent more
-- chemical every day for the life of the field.
--
-- AND THE ONLY REFUSAL BRANCH REFUSES ON CONCENTRATION, NEVER ON SHORTFALL.
-- The one comparison in the function is `weightPct > maxWtPct`. Sweeping
-- methanol, the shortfall grows monotonically with the need, 10.70 degF at a
-- 60 degF subcooling to 61.40 degF at 170 degF, and ok is TRUE for every row
-- of that. The single refused row is refused for asking 71.18 weight percent,
-- not for being the worst answer in the table. The ceiling itself is measured
-- in Hammerschmidt coordinates: 70 weight percent methanol is 170.05 degF of
-- Hammerschmidt depression and 108.62 degF of Nielsen-Bucklin, so the engine
-- will design for far more subcooling than the chemistry it checks against can
-- deliver.
--
-- THREE CONVENTIONS THAT DO NOT TRAVEL, AND THE TIER ENDS ON THEM.
--
--   THREE VALUES OF ONE CONSTANT. The engine carries Hammerschmidt k = 2335 on
--   all four inhibitors; the oracle reaches 2334.6 through 1297 x 1.8; and
--   129.6 x 18.015 = 2334.744 is the value that makes the module's own two
--   relations meet in the dilute limit. The gate compares Hammerschmidt at
--   5e-4 relative and Nielsen-Bucklin at 1e-9, five and a half orders of
--   magnitude apart, which is the disagreement absorbed as a tolerance.
--
--   `leanWtPct` IS A WEIGHT PERCENT IN THE MASS GROSS-UP AND A VOLUME PERCENT
--   ONE LINE LATER IN THE DENSITY BLEND. `injectionRate` grosses the pure mass
--   up by 100/lean, which is a mass basis, and then blends the stream density
--   as (rho_inh x lean + rho_water x (100 - lean))/100, which is a volume
--   basis. On the capstone methanol it is worth 0.307 percent on the rate, and
--   more on a leaner stream.
--
--   AND `reliable` IS A PRINT SWITCH ON CONCENTRATION, NOT AN ACCURACY CLAIM.
--   It is `weightPct <= 25`, and the disagreement it looks like it describes
--   grows smoothly straight through that cut: at exactly 25.0 weight percent
--   methanol the two relations are already 2.0293 degF apart, 9.11 percent of
--   the Nielsen-Bucklin value, with the flag still TRUE. Crossing it moves the
--   headline by 2.0269 degF while neither relation moves at all. For a glycol
--   it is stranger still: `nielsenBucklinF` is null, so the flag flips to
--   false, the basis stays hammerschmidt, and `recommendedF` stays the same
--   number the engine has just called unreliable.
--
-- THE CHECK THE MODULE DECLINES TO RUN WOULD RETURN THE IDENTICAL NUMBER.
-- Every inhibitor carries the same k, and the Hammerschmidt inverse fixes a
-- MOLE FRACTION that contains no molecular weight at all, so all four fluids
-- sized to one requirement land on one mole fraction and one Nielsen-Bucklin
-- answer. The capstone methanol and MEG doses, 38.5919 and 54.9037 weight
-- percent, are the same 0.261096 mole fraction to the last bit a double
-- carries, and Nielsen-Bucklin on either gives 39.2154 degF. The oracle
-- publishes a `nielsenBucklinF` for all four fluids in all 24 golden rows, so
-- the check the module suppresses sits in the very file it is validated
-- against.
-- ============================================================================

insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
values ('flowassurance', 'advanced', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-joule-thomson","title":"Joule Thomson","lesson_keys":["l01-gas-cools-as-it-expands","l02-where-the-term-belongs","l03-applied-undamped","l04-the-verdict-flips","l05-what-the-damping-would-be"]},{"key":"m02-a-refusal-that-is-not","title":"A Refusal That Is Not","lesson_keys":["l01-a-nan-caught-and-dropped","l02-what-dropping-a-term-does","l03-refusing-against-continuing","l04-what-ok-means-here"]},{"key":"m03-the-inhibitor","title":"The Inhibitor","lesson_keys":["l01-what-depression-is","l02-hammerschmidt","l03-sizing-the-dose","l04-the-injection-rate","l05-what-the-dose-assumes"]},{"key":"m04-sized-one-way-checked-another","title":"Sized One Way, Checked Another","lesson_keys":["l01-two-correlations-one-dose","l02-the-check-that-is-never-compared","l03-how-short-is-short","l04-no-check-at-all-for-meg","l05-what-a-passing-check-proved"]},{"key":"m05-ceilings-and-conventions","title":"Ceilings and Conventions","lesson_keys":["l01-a-ceiling-in-the-wrong-coordinates","l02-weight-percent-against-volume-percent","l03-the-reference-nobody-accepts","l04-which-conventions-travel"]},{"key":"m06-the-expert-reading","title":"The Expert Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'flowassurance' and tier = 'advanced';

insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 1, 'steadyStateProfile takes inletPsia, outletPsia and jtCoeffFPerPsi. Which of those three does it compute for itself?', '["The coefficient, which it fits from the heat capacity of 0.620 Btu/(lb degF) and the bore before it applies the term.", "None of them. The drop is the inlet less the outlet and the coefficient is a fluid property out of an equation of state.", "The pressure drop, which it recovers from a friction traverse walked along the same 60000.0 ft the station table already covers.", "Both pressures, which fall out of the arrival temperature once the 21 stations have been built."]'::jsonb, 1, 'jtCoeffFPerPsi defaults to zero, so a caller who omits it gets a full station table and ok true with the effect silently absent.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 2, 'TEACHING LINE AKASO SPUR carries 2400.0 psia at the inlet, 1500.0 psia at the outlet and a Joule-Thomson coefficient of 0.0280 degF/psi. What term does the engine form from those?', '["25.2000000000 degF, the whole coefficient against the whole 900.0 psi drop.", "14.5616907938 degF, because the module divides the product by the damping factor of 1.730568266886 before it subtracts anything.", "10.6383092062 degF, the part of the product that survives the relaxation over the remaining length of the line.", "Nothing, because no published case sets a pressure and the term is disabled where no golden covers it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine forms jtDrop as jtCoeff times dp times the fraction of the length travelled, and at the outlet that fraction is one.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 3, 'At each station the engine forms the ambient and adds the inlet excess decayed by exp(-x/Lc). What does it then do with the Joule-Thomson term?', '["It weights the term by (1 - exp(-ntu))/ntu first, which on the teaching line is the reciprocal of 1.730568266886.", "It adds the term to the ambient, which raises the floor the fluid relaxes toward above the 45.00 degF seabed.", "It subtracts the coefficient times the drop times the travelled fraction, outside the exponential, so the whole term comes off at the arrival.", "It takes the term off the 195.00 degF inlet before the exponential runs, so the term decays over the relaxation length of 49209.01299043 ft with everything else."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The subtraction sits outside the exponential, so no relaxation ever reaches it and the arrival loses the full 25.2000000000 degF.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 4, 'Cooling produced at distance x has L less x of line still to travel, and it decays over that distance exactly as the inlet excess does. What weighting does that put on a source spread evenly along the line?', '["exp(-ntu) itself, 0.295440199685 on the teaching line, since the term is carried the whole length just as the inlet excess is.", "One half, because the average station has travelled half the line and the pressure drop is taken as linear in distance.", "None at all. Cooling produced along the line reaches the outlet unchanged, which is why the whole product is the right thing to subtract.", "(1 - exp(-ntu))/ntu, which is below one for every positive ntu."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The factor tends to one as ntu tends to zero and falls toward one over ntu as ntu grows, so a long line gives most of the cooling back.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 5, 'On the teaching line ntu is 1.219288832549 and exp(-ntu) is 0.295440199685. What reaches the far end of the 25.2000000000 degF Joule-Thomson term, and what does the engine take off instead?', '["14.5616907938 degF should reach it and the engine takes off the whole 25.2000000000 degF, which is 10.6383092062 degF in excess.", "The whole term multiplied by exp(-ntu), which is 0.295440199685 here, since the cooling decays over the whole line before it arrives.", "The whole term reaches it either way, and the 10.6383092062 degF is the heat the seabed returns rather than a modelling error.", "25.2000000000 degF should reach it and the engine applies 14.5616907938 degF, so the shipped answer is the conservative one of the two."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The damping factor ntu/(1 - exp(-ntu)) is 1.730568266886 on this line, and the engine term is exactly that multiple of the damped one.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 6, 'The teaching line is measured against a 71.00 degF flowing hydrate boundary, which is a laboratory input. What do the three ways of applying the Joule-Thomson term say about the verdict?', '["All three arrive outside the region, and the disagreement is only worth 10.6383092062 degF of margin rather than a change of side.", "Only the heat loss arrival clears the boundary, because the damped term of 14.5616907938 degF is already enough to cross it.", "Heat loss alone arrives at 89.316029952695 degF and the damped term at 74.754339158867 degF, both outside, while the engine returns 64.116029952695 degF, inside.", "The verdict cannot be read from any of them, since the engine refuses a profile once the arrival falls below a supplied hydrate temperature."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The margins are 18.3160299527 degF, 3.7543391589 degF and -6.8839700473 degF, so one damping factor decides whether the line is safe or hydrating.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 7, 'The correctly damped arrival clears the 71.00 degF boundary by 3.7543391589 degF. What is the honest way to report that?', '["As proof that the shipped engine is conservative, since the number it returns keeps a designer further from a boundary a laboratory drew.", "As a real margin that is not a comfortable one, so the correct answer is a small pass rather than a clean one.", "As a margin no field instrument could resolve, so the damped arrival and the engine arrival of 64.116029952695 degF are indistinguishable in practice.", "As a comfortable clearance, because it is the same order as the 18.3160299527 degF the heat loss arrival keeps."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine is wrong about the verdict and the line is still not far from genuine risk, which is two separate statements and both belong in a review.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 8, 'On which lines does applying the Joule-Thomson term undamped barely matter?', '["Lines with a large pressure drop, where the term dominates the heat loss and the weighting is lost in the size of the answer.", "Buried lines, because the trench resistance of 0.2835082263 hr ft degF/Btu per foot slows the relaxation the weighting depends on.", "Lines whose ntu is large, since the damping factor falls toward one over ntu and a factor near one changes very little.", "Lines much shorter than their own relaxation length, where ntu is small and the damping factor is near one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The factor is already 1.730568266886 at an ntu of 1.219288832549, which is where an insulated subsea line is designed to sit.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 9, 'Take the teaching line from 60000.0 ft to 90000.0 ft and nothing else moves. What happens to the two halves of the answer?', '["The heat loss arrival falls from 89.3160299527 degF to 69.0877170663 degF while the subtracted term stays 25.2000000000 degF, so the engine returns 43.8877170663 degF.", "Both halves fall together, since the term is formed from the pressure drop and the drop grows with the length of the line.", "The heat loss arrival falls and the term shrinks with it, which is why the engine arrival stays above the 45.00 degF seabed at every length.", "The heat loss arrival is unchanged at 89.3160299527 degF because ntu is already past one, and only the term moves."]'::jsonb, 0, 'ntu goes from 1.2192888325 to 1.8289332488 and the engine arrival crosses the 45.00 degF seabed the line is losing heat to.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 10, 'At 180000.0 ft steadyStateProfile returns 23.6681207564 degF with ok true. What does uForArrivalTemp, in the same file, do when it is asked for that temperature?', '["It returns the U that reaches it, 0.651078288819 Btu/(hr ft2 degF), since the inverse and the forward profile are the same exponential.", "It accepts it and reports the implied ntu of 3.6578664976 alongside, which is the same ntu the forward profile printed.", "It refuses, with the reason that a line cannot arrive above ambient no matter how well it is insulated and the target has to be above it.", "It refuses, on the ground that the fluid already enters below the target, which is the other of the two refusals it carries."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two functions in one file take opposite positions on the same temperature, and neither of them knows the other has been asked.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 11, 'A profile is run on the teaching line with no pressures, then again with the inlet, the outlet and the coefficient set. Which of the relaxation length, ntu, exp(-ntu) and the heat loss arrival moves between the two runs?', '["ntu and exp(-ntu) both move, because the term changes the temperature the line relaxes from at every station past the inlet.", "None of the four. The term is taken off after the exponential has been formed, so only the arrival moves.", "The relaxation length moves, since it is m Cp over U pi D and the Joule-Thomson term enters the same energy balance.", "All four move, which is why the two runs cannot be compared station by station."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The term is subtracted after the exponential has been formed, so it touches nothing that goes into 49209.01299043 ft or 1.219288832549.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 12, 'A reviewer audits the Joule-Thomson term by checking its dimensions. What do they find?', '["A degF per psi against a psi that gives a pressure rather than a temperature, which is the sign the term was mis-scaled.", "A factor of 1.730568266886 with no units attached to it, which is what a dimensional check is for.", "A mismatch between the 900.0 psi drop and the 2400.0 psia inlet, since one is a difference and the other an absolute pressure.", "Nothing wrong at all, because a missing weighting on an otherwise correct product has no units to give it away."]'::jsonb, 3, 'degF per psi against psi is degF, both inputs are the caller''s own, and the arithmetic is unimpeachable at every step.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 13, 'No golden case in this course sets a pressure anywhere. What does that mean for the Joule-Thomson term?', '["It is carried at the catalogue default until a case overrides it, which is why the published arrivals are cooler than heat loss alone.", "It is validated indirectly, since the arrival at 105600.0 ft of 43.357693442744 degF agrees with the oracle to 2.091692e-9 relative.", "It is exactly zero in every published case, so no golden shows it and the independent oracle never sees any of it.", "It is refused, because the module will not build a station table it cannot fill a pressure column for."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published profile at 105600.0 ft returns a pressure column reading n/a at every station rather than hiding the absence.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 14, 'A caller passes an inlet pressure and a coefficient to steadyStateProfile and forgets the outlet. What comes back?', '["The drop stays zero, so the arrival is the heat loss answer of 89.3160299527 degF, with ok true and no note.", "A refusal, since the module forms the drop as the inlet less the outlet and will not accept half a pair.", "The engine arrival of 64.116029952695 degF, because the outlet defaults to the ambient pressure at the far end of the line.", "An arrival between the two, with the term applied over whatever part of the length the single pressure covers."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing about pressures is refused here: a drop of zero, a coefficient of zero and any length all come back with ok true and no note.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-joule-thomson', 15, 'Past 90000.0 ft the engine arrival on the teaching line sits below the seabed the line is losing heat to. Why is that not conservatism?', '["Because a sub-ambient arrival is refused elsewhere in the module, so the profile has already left the range it was validated on.", "Because the module models relaxation toward ambient correctly, and the Joule-Thomson term is applied where that relaxation cannot reach it.", "Because an inlet 20.0 degF below the 40.0 degF ambient comes back ok true and arrives at 39.5203295082 degF, warming toward its surroundings rather than cooling past them.", "Because the seabed at 45.00 degF is an input rather than a computed floor, and no engine enforces an input."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine knows fluid relaxes toward ambient, which is exactly why an arrival of 21.9024942545 degF at 210000.0 ft is a modelling error and not a margin.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 1, 'burialResistance is acosh(2H/D) over 2 pi k. Where on the published 8.625 in coated pipe does it stop having a value, and why?', '["At a burial of zero, because a pipe lying on the seabed has no soil above it and the shape factor divides by that thickness.", "It never stops. The function clamps a shallow trench to the pipe lying on the bottom and returns the floor value of 0.0000000000 hr ft degF/Btu per foot.", "At a burial of 0.35937500 ft to the centreline, because below half the coated diameter 2H/D falls under 1 and acosh has no real value there.", "At a soil conductivity of zero, since the term is exactly inverse in k and nothing else in the expression can leave the reals."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 3.00 ft the same call returns 0.3728922550 on a 2H/D of 8.34782609, and at 0.30 ft the 2H/D is 0.83478261 and the answer is a NaN.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 2, 'overallU builds a list of resistances and refuses the call if any entry in that list is not finite. Why does a NaN burial term not reach that refusal?', '["The burial term is pushed only when Number.isFinite says it is a number, and that guard runs before the refusal, so the NaN never joins the list being inspected.", "The refusal inspects only the layer entries, because the films and the ground term are added after the check has already run.", "The burial term is converted to zero before the list is assembled, which is finite, so the refusal has nothing left to catch.", "The refusal fires on the total resistance rather than on the entries, and a total that is a NaN still tests as a positive number in JavaScript."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The call succeeds one term short: 4 entries where 5 were asked for, with ok true, an error field of none and no note anywhere.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 3, 'The published buried stack is computed with the trench entered as 3.0 ft and then as 0.3 ft. What comes back?', '["A refusal on the second call, since the module already refuses a layer whose outside diameter is not larger than its inside.", "Two coefficients that bracket the truth, because a shallower trench is modelled with less soil rather than with none of it at all.", "0.7455927364 on both, because the burial term is floored at the pipe lying on the bottom rather than dropped from the stack.", "0.7455927364 Btu/(hr ft2 degF) on 5 terms and 1.3348791131 on 4 terms, both with ok true and no note."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The error in U is 79.035960 percent, and the vanished ground term was carrying 44.145299 percent of the correct stack.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 4, 'What makes the swallowed answer of 1.3348791131 Btu/(hr ft2 degF) particularly hard to catch by inspection?', '["It carries a note saying the burial was approximated, which a reader takes as evidence the term was considered.", "It is the engine''s own published insulated build, the same pipe with no trench at all, matching to 0.0000e+0 relative.", "It is far enough from 0.7455927364 that a reviewer assumes a different pipe was entered rather than a different trench.", "It sits between the bare and the insulated builds, so no single sanity check on the size of a coefficient can bracket it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A plausible number is the one kind of wrong answer no reviewer catches, and this one is exactly the U that pipe would have lying exposed on the seabed.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 5, 'A dropped resistance can only ever move U in one direction. Which way, and why is that direction still a design error?', '["U comes back low, since a shorter resistance list divides the same conductance among fewer terms and each is then understated.", "It depends on which term was dropped, because a film resistance and a ground resistance enter the stack with opposite signs.", "U comes back high, so the arrival reads colder and the cooldown shorter, and an answer wrong in the safe direction still buys insulation and inhibitor the line does not need.", "U comes back high, and that is acceptable, since every flow assurance margin is conservative by construction and a cold arrival never costs anything."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a cooldown it also sends people to a valve earlier than the physics requires, which is a real cost on a real deck.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 6, 'The same guard swallows a trench on the published pipe and on TEACHING LINE AKASO SPUR, and the two errors are very different sizes. What sets the difference?', '["The coated diameter, since a 16.750 in pipe has a larger surface area and therefore a larger absolute error in U.", "How much of the stack the ground term was carrying: 44.145299 percent on the published pipe and 32.14809253 percent on the teaching line.", "The number of layers, because the teaching line has a foam layer and a weight coat and the published pipe has foam alone.", "The soil conductivity, which is the wet seabed value of 1.2 Btu/(hr ft degF) on the published trench and lower on the teaching line, so the ground term is worth more there."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One guard, two lines: the error in U is 79.035960 percent on the published pipe and 47.379792 percent on the teaching line, where U reads 0.6675904532 against 0.4529728566.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 7, 'The dropped ground term is not the end of the calculation. What does it do to the relaxation length on the published buried stack at 120000.0 lb/hr and Cp 0.5?', '["It falls from 52983.47772700 ft to 28308.04522908 ft, so the line is computed as relaxing toward the seabed in a little over half the distance it really does.", "It is unchanged, because the relaxation length is m Cp over U pi D and the mass rate and the bore both stayed the same.", "It rises, since a higher U means the fluid holds its excess over a longer distance before the sea can take it.", "It falls by 44.145299 percent, which is the share the ground term was carrying, because the relaxation length is linear in the total resistance that went missing from the stack."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Relaxation length is exactly inverse in U, and since ntu is the length over that number the error then sits inside an exponential.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 8, 'cooldownTime reads both of its mass slots as (contents?.massLbPerFt || 0). What happens when one slot is a NaN and what happens when both are?', '["Both bad returns zero hours and one bad returns a refusal, because a single missing mass is the ambiguous case and the module declines it.", "Either case is refused, since the guard tests each slot separately before the total heat capacity is formed from the two of them.", "Both bad and one bad return the same answer of 4.6627248553 hr, because a NaN mass is replaced with the catalogue steel default of 490 lbm/ft3.", "Both bad is refused for want of a heat capacity, and one bad returns ok true with hours, a time constant and a full station table."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The guard is !(mcp > 0) on the TOTAL, so it only fires when both slots have gone, and one bad slot comes back short by 72.534444 percent at 1.2806433091 hr against 4.6627248553 hr.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 9, 'The swallowed trench and the swallowed cooldown mass are the same NaN-is-falsy failure. Which of the two is sharper, and what makes it so?', '["The trench one, since a coefficient is used by three separate consumers and a no-touch time is read by one operator on one occasion.", "The trench one, because 79.035960 percent is a larger error than 72.534444 percent and the size of the error is what a review has to catch.", "The mass one, because overallU returns a resistances array a caller can count while cooldownTime returns ok, hours, a time constant and stations, and nothing countable.", "Neither is sharper. Both return ok true with no note, and a failure a caller cannot see is a failure a caller cannot see."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Counting 4 entries where 5 were asked for catches the trench. No care taken with the cooldown return finds the missing mass at any effort.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 10, 'One module, one class of bad input, three different policies. What are they?', '["A refusal, a clamped value at the nearest physical limit, and a note attached to an answer that was computed anyway.", "A refusal object with a written reason, a term caught and dropped with ok true, and a bare NaN returned in place of any object at all.", "A refusal, a warning field on the return, and an error thrown to the caller for the cases the module cannot describe.", "A refusal on geometry, a refusal on fluid properties, and a refusal on temperature pairs, which between them cover every input the module takes."]'::jsonb, 1, 'relaxationLengthFt returns a NaN on a zero U, and pipeMassLbPerFt and contentsMassLbPerFt do the same, so a caller who learned the policy from the layer refusals learned the wrong one.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 11, 'What does ok true actually certify in these returns?', '["That every input handed in was resolved and used, which is what the layer refusals were written to guarantee across the whole module.", "That the answer is inside the range the published goldens cover, since the harness compares each return against an independent oracle.", "That no NaN survives anywhere in the returned object, which is the discipline the conductivity helper header states in as many words.", "That the function reached the end of its own code."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is not evidence that a trench, a mass or a temperature pair was used, and every result in this module passes a branch written on ok alone.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 12, 'cooldownTime is asked for a target below ambient. What does it return, and why is that return worth studying?', '["Hours of Infinity with ok true, a time constant of 8.6008917110 hr that is still meaningful, an empty station list and a written note saying the line settles at ambient.", "A refusal with a written reason, which is the same shape the module uses for a start temperature already below ambient.", "A very large finite number of hours, since the exponential approaches ambient without reaching it and the solver stops at its iteration limit.", "Hours of Infinity with ok false, because a limit that cannot be reached is the definition of a refusal in this module."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is the shape the missing branch should have had: the same function returns -4.6959175559 hr with no note when the target sits above the start.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 13, 'A profile is given an inlet pressure and no outlet. What does the station pressure column show?', '["A column reading n/a at every station, which is the same honest answer a call with neither pressure returns.", "A column falling linearly to the ambient pressure at the far end, since the missing outlet takes the seabed value as its default.", "A fully populated column flat at 2400.0 psia, none of which the line ever had, with the Joule-Thomson term silently zero.", "A refusal, because the drop is formed as the inlet less the outlet and a half specified pair cannot produce one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The drop guard reads both pressures and the column guard reads the inlet alone, and the n/a column was the only tell that the term had not been applied.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 14, 'Forgetting the inlet pressure and forgetting the outlet pressure are not the same mistake. Why not?', '["Forgetting the inlet is refused with a written reason while forgetting the outlet is not, so only one of the two ever gets as far as producing an arrival temperature.", "Forgetting the inlet leaves an honest n/a column and forgetting the outlet leaves a plausible one, and the outlet is the pressure more likely to be missing.", "Forgetting the inlet halves the pressure drop and forgetting the outlet doubles it, so the two errors are of opposite sign and of roughly comparable size on the arrival.", "Forgetting the inlet changes the arrival and forgetting the outlet does not, because the term is anchored on the inlet pressure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both return the same arrival of 89.3160299527 degF with ok true and no note, and only the pressure column tells the two apart.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-a-refusal-that-is-not', 15, 'Neither dropped term can be caught from the returned object in every case. What has to be checked instead?', '["The ok field together with the error field, since between them they record every input the module chose not to use.", "The station tables, because a dropped resistance or a dropped mass leaves a visible discontinuity in the temperatures they print.", "The agreement with the golden oracle, which is where a swallowed term would show up as a relative difference outside the harness tolerance.", "The inputs, before the call: the trench against half the coated diameter, and each mass against the geometry that produced it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'pipeMassLbPerFt and contentsMassLbPerFt return a bare NaN rather than a refusal, so the check has to happen where the NaN is made rather than where it lands.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 1, 'Depression and subcooling are the two quantities a dose is argued from. Which of them does hydrateInhibition compute?', '["Both, since the module carries the Hammerschmidt constant and the water molecular weight of 18.015000 and needs nothing else.", "Subcooling only, which it forms from the arrival temperature the thermal module hands it and its own hydrate correlation.", "Depression only. Subcooling is a hydrate temperature from a laboratory less a fluid temperature, and the module header says it does not compute the first of those.", "Neither. Both arrive as inputs, and the module contributes the injection rate and nothing more to the argument."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every subcooling in this course is a laboratory number subtracted from an engine number, so the answer is only as good as the half nobody computed.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 2, 'The Hammerschmidt depression is K W over M times 100 less W. What is W?', '["The inhibitor weight percent in the AQUEOUS phase, which is the strength of the water rather than of anything else flowing.", "The inhibitor weight percent of the total produced stream, gas and condensate and water together, at the point the dose is measured.", "The volume percent of inhibitor in the injected lean stream, which is why the lean strength is carried as a separate input.", "The mole fraction of inhibitor in the water, which is what makes the relation comparable with Nielsen-Bucklin term by term."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A line making little water and injecting a modest rate is a strong aqueous phase and a weak stream at once, and only one of the two belongs in the relation.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 3, 'All four catalogue inhibitors ship the same k of 2335.0. What then separates them under Hammerschmidt at one concentration?', '["Their liquid densities, 6.6000 lb/gal for methanol against 9.4000 for TEG, which enter the depression through the aqueous phase.", "Their Nielsen-Bucklin availability flags, since the reported depression is whichever relation the catalogue makes available for that fluid.", "Nothing separates them. One constant and one relation give one depression, and the fluids differ only in what they cost to inject.", "Molecular weight alone, 32.04 for methanol against 150.17 for TEG."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 20.0 weight percent the four read 18.2194132335, 9.4047043660, 5.5008480965 and 3.8872611041 degF, which is the ordering of one over M and nothing else.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 4, 'HAMMERSCHMIDT_RELIABLE_WT_PCT is 25.0 weight percent. What does the engine do at a concentration above it?', '["It refuses, with a written reason naming the concentration and the reliability limit it crossed.", "It neither clamps nor refuses. It sets reliable false, computes the number anyway and attaches a note.", "It clamps the reported depression to the value the relation gives at 25.0 weight percent, which is 24.2925509779 degF for methanol.", "It switches the sizing relation to Nielsen-Bucklin, so a dose above that line is chosen by a different correlation from one below it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The flip above the line changes which relation is printed as recommendedF, and it does not change the concentration anybody picked.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 5, 'At 25.0 weight percent methanol the engine reports reliable true. What is the disagreement between its two relations at exactly that concentration?', '["Zero, which is what the reliability line marks: the point below which the two relations are indistinguishable.", "0.0560733386 degF, the spread the two relations hold from the dilute limit up to the reliability line.", "2.0292736312 degF, which is 9.114892 percent of the Nielsen-Bucklin value.", "15.0582616180 degF, the spread that makes the flag flip on the next row of the sweep."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The relations already disagree at 5.0 weight percent, where the ratio is 1.0148358155, and the gap simply widens with no step in it anywhere.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 6, 'What is the reliable flag actually reporting?', '["Which side of a fixed concentration the design sits on, which is a print switch rather than a claim about accuracy.", "How far apart the two relations are on this row, expressed as a tolerance the engine is willing to stand behind.", "Whether the Nielsen-Bucklin relation was available for the fluid, which is why it reads false on every glycol design.", "Whether the concentration came out of the inverse or was supplied directly, since only one of the two routes has been validated."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The test is weightPct at or below 25, and the disagreement it looks like it describes grows smoothly straight through that cut.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 7, 'Solving the Hammerschmidt relation for a concentration gives a weight percent for any depression somebody names. What does the module do about that?', '["It refuses any depression above the largest one the 24 published rows cover, which is 72.8776529338 degF for methanol.", "It lets the inverse run, since the expression climbs toward 100 weight percent and never reaches it, so no arithmetic ever breaks.", "It switches to the Nielsen-Bucklin inverse above the reliability line, which is bounded and therefore refuses on its own once the mole fraction saturates.", "It carries MAX_PRACTICAL_WT_PCT at 70.0 weight percent, because past there the aqueous phase is barely water."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The header says deep subcooling is solved with insulation, heating or displacing the line, not by pushing a concentration further.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 8, 'TEACHING LINE AKASO SPUR asks for 36.00 degF of shut-in subcooling with a 5.00 degF safety margin. What does inhibitionRequirement size against?', '["36.00 degF, with the margin applied afterwards as a multiplier on the concentration that comes back.", "41.00 degF, the sum of the two, because safetyMarginF is added to subcoolingF before the inverse is called.", "36.00 degF, since a safety margin is a reporting field and does not enter the sizing arithmetic at all.", "41.00 degF, and the same sum is then applied a second time inside the injection rate as a gross-up on the mass."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A fixed margin in degF costs progressively more concentration the further out the design already sits, because the relation is not linear.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 9, 'inhibitionRequirement returns neededDepressionF, weightPct, depressionCheck, rate and a top level ok. What is that ok?', '["A conjunction of the sizing, the check and the rate, so it is false whenever any of the three failed.", "A flag on the concentration, true while the design sits below MAX_PRACTICAL_WT_PCT of 70.0 weight percent.", "The injection rate''s own ok, which says a rate could be computed and reports nothing about the depression.", "The depression check''s ok, which is why a design that misses its subcooling comes back with ok false and an error."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A design that misses its subcooling and one that hits it return exactly the same ok, so the field carries no verdict at all.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 10, 'injectionRate is a mass balance on the produced water. Where does the lean strength enter it?', '["As a gross-up: the pure inhibitor mass is multiplied by 100 over the lean strength, because the injected stream is not pure.", "As a correction on the target concentration, since a weaker lean stream cannot reach as high a strength in the water.", "As a multiplier on the produced water rate of 420.0 bbl/d, because the water the lean stream brings with it joins the water already there.", "It does not enter the mass balance. It appears only in the stream density that turns pounds a day into barrels a day."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sized at 36.0035520084 weight percent methanol from a 96.00 weight percent lean stream, 82766.408593 lb/day of pure inhibitor becomes 86215.008951 lb/day of stream.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 11, 'Methanol on the teaching line duty needs 307.7753251096 bbl/d and MEG on the same duty needs 466.5311621077 bbl/d. What opens that gap?', '["The lean strengths alone, 96.00 weight percent against 89.00, since the gross-up is the only place the two designs differ.", "The Nielsen-Bucklin check, which trims the methanol dose and is unavailable to trim the MEG one, so the MEG rate is reported untrimmed.", "The produced water rate, which is read at the cold point for methanol and as a design maximum for MEG.", "Two properties that do not move together: molecular weight sets the pounds the water needs and density sets the gallons they occupy."]'::jsonb, 3, 'MEG is sized at 52.1503646614 weight percent and blends to 9.1944000000 lb/gal against 6.6696000000 for methanol, so both conversions run against it.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 12, 'injectionRate refuses three things. Which refusal is the one worth having?', '["A produced water rate of zero, which is refused because a mass balance on no water has no denominator to work from.", "A lean strength no stronger than the concentration it has to produce, refused with a sentence saying it cannot get there however much is injected.", "A target concentration outside 0 to 100 weight percent, which is refused before the gross-up can produce a negative mass.", "A stream density below the water density, which is refused because a blend cannot be lighter than either of the two components."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A lean stream weaker than its target is not a rate problem, no pumping fixes it, and the module says so rather than returning a large number.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 13, 'The balance puts the whole injected stream into the aqueous phase. What does the catalogue itself say about methanol that the balance does not model?', '["That it freezes at concentrations above the practical ceiling, which is the reason the ceiling sits at 70.0 weight percent.", "That its Hammerschmidt constant of 2335.0 was fitted on fresh water and drifts on a produced brine.", "That it is lost to the gas and the condensate, which is what makes recovery hard and is usually why a project chooses glycol instead.", "That its density of 6.6000 lb/gal is quoted at standard conditions and rises through an umbilical at seabed temperature."]'::jsonb, 2, 'None of that loss is modelled, so a methanol design stopping at the returned rate is short by whatever the vapour and the condensate take.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 14, 'Produced water is usually saline and salt inhibits hydrates. How does the module handle that?', '["It applies a default brine correction to the water density before the mass balance, which is why the water density is an exposed input.", "It folds an average salinity into the Hammerschmidt constant, which is why the three carried values of that constant differ slightly.", "It refuses a design on saline water, since the relation it uses was fitted on fresh water and the header says it will not extrapolate.", "It leaves salinity out of the balance and says so, because the depression salt gives depends on the ions present and is a flash calculation rather than a constant."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ignoring salinity over-injects, and the module states the omission rather than approximating it, which is the difference between a limit and a hidden error.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-inhibitor', 15, 'hammerschmidtDepression is handed a weight percent at or above 100. What does it return?', '["A bare NaN, not a refusal object with a written reason.", "A refusal object with the sentence naming the concentration and the range the relation accepts, in the same shape overallU uses for a bad layer.", "The depression at the practical ceiling of 70.0 weight percent, clamped, since no aqueous phase can be stronger than pure inhibitor.", "Infinity, which is the limit of the expression as the concentration approaches 100 and is a meaningful answer for a caller to test."]'::jsonb, 0, 'weightPctForDepression does the same on a depression that is not positive, so an untested NaN out of either one travels downstream unchallenged.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 1, 'Three things happen inside one inhibitionRequirement call. Which relation does each of them use?', '["Both relations pick the concentration and the lower of the two is taken, which is why a design above the reliability line is the conservative one.", "The Hammerschmidt inverse picks the concentration, depression is then run on that concentration and reports Nielsen-Bucklin above the reliability line, and injectionRate turns it into barrels.", "Nielsen-Bucklin picks the concentration because it is the relation the module prefers, and Hammerschmidt is retained only for the printed comparison.", "One relation does all three, since the check would be meaningless if it were run with a correlation the sizing had not used."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing in the function reads recommendedF back against neededDepressionF, so the second opinion is collected and then left unread.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 2, 'On the teaching line need of 41.00 degF the requirement returns weightPct 36.0035520084. What sits in depressionCheck beside it?', '["hammerschmidtF and nielsenBucklinF that both read 41.0000000000 degF, since the concentration was chosen to satisfy the need exactly.", "A single recommendedF of 41.0000000000 degF, because the check reports whichever relation the sizing used and no other.", "hammerschmidtF of 35.6195882812 degF and nielsenBucklinF of 41.0000000000 degF, the sized relation being the lower of the two up there.", "hammerschmidtF of 41.0000000000 degF and nielsenBucklinF of 35.6195882812 degF, with the second reported as recommendedF."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sized depression and the delivered depression sit one field apart in the same returned object, and ok reads true across both.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 3, 'The teaching line dose is short of its 41.00 degF need by 5.3804117188 degF. What does that do to the 5.00 degF safety margin the design asked for?', '["It consumes the whole margin and 0.3804117188 degF more, so the design falls short even against the bare 36.00 degF subcooling.", "It uses most of the margin and leaves a little of it, since the shortfall is 13.122955 percent of the need rather than of the margin.", "It leaves the margin intact, because the margin was added before the inverse ran and is therefore already inside the sized concentration.", "It cannot be compared with the margin, since one is a depression in degF and the other a concentration in weight percent."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Percentages of a depression are not percentages of a margin, and a hydrate verdict is decided on the margin.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 4, 'Inverting Nielsen-Bucklin instead for the same 41.00 degF gives 39.8251780234 weight percent methanol. What does the honest dose cost on the teaching line?', '["Nothing extra, because the injection rate is set by the 420.0 bbl/d of produced water and not by the concentration in it.", "3.8216260150 bbl/d more, which is the difference between the two concentrations carried straight through to the rate.", "362.0655724349 bbl/d against 307.7753251096, which is 54.2902473253 bbl/d and 17.639571 percent more methanol.", "Twice the rate, since the concentration difference of 3.8216260150 weight percent falls in the steep part of the gross-up."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is the size of the finding in the only currency a facility cares about, a pump and a tank.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 5, 'Below a need of about 20.0 degF the shortfall between the sized depression and the checked one reads exactly zero. What produced that zero?', '["The two relations genuinely agree in that range, which is what the reliability line at 25.0 weight percent was drawn along the concentration axis to mark.", "The concentration came out of the Hammerschmidt inverse and the check ran Hammerschmidt forward on it, so the two operations undo one another.", "The check is skipped below the reliability line, so the field is filled with the need itself rather than with a computed depression.", "The safety margin absorbs the disagreement at low needs, and only runs out once the design climbs past the reliability line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At a 15.0 degF need the requirement sizes 17.0691859639 weight percent and reports 15.0000000000 degF back, which restates its own input.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 6, 'Between a need of 20.0 degF and a need of 25.0 degF the shortfall jumps from zero to 2.1423672457 degF in one row. What stepped?', '["The chemistry, since 25.0 weight percent is where a dilute solution result stops describing the aqueous phase at all.", "The safety margin, which the function stops adding once the concentration passes the reliability line and starts reporting a shortfall instead.", "The inverse, which switches to a bounded form above the reliability line and therefore returns a lower concentration than the need requires.", "The reporting rule. The sized concentration crossed 25.0 weight percent, moving from 21.5337052221 to 25.5420918367, so basis changed relation."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing about the fluid changed at the crossing, and the first row whose check carries any information is the first row where two different relations meet on one concentration.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 7, 'Across the sweep of needs the shortfall is positive on every row above the crossing. Can a design in that region ever over-deliver?', '["No. The sizing relation is the higher of the two at every concentration, so a design in that region is always short and never long.", "Yes, on any row where reliable comes back false, because the engine then reports the larger of its two relations as the delivered depression.", "Yes, once the concentration passes 70.0 weight percent, where the two relations cross and the ordering between them reverses.", "It varies with the fluid, since the ordering of the two relations depends on the molecular weight each of them is written against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'In percent terms it more than doubles between needs of 25.0 and 60.0 degF, from 8.569469 to 17.827312, and the direction never changes.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 8, 'The same 41.00 degF need sized in MEG returns weightPct 52.1503646614 and a depressionCheck reading recommendedF 41.0000000000 degF. What has the check established?', '["That MEG delivers its need exactly, which is why the glycols are preferred where a design has to be defended.", "That MEG sits below the reliability line at that concentration, so the Hammerschmidt value is the trustworthy one of the two relations to report.", "Nothing. nielsenBucklinF is null for MEG, so recommendedF is the sizing relation printed back and the shortfall reads 0.0000000000 degF.", "That the check ran and agreed, since a null spreadF beside it means the two relations differed by nothing worth printing at that concentration."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On the methanol return a reader sees two numbers and can notice they differ, and on the MEG return there is one number that equals the need.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 9, 'Why do methanol, MEG, DEG and TEG sized to one depression all land on the same mole fraction?', '["Because the catalogue densities of 6.6000 and 9.3000 lb/gal cancel against the molecular weights when the concentration is converted to moles.", "The Hammerschmidt inverse fixes w over M times 100 less w at the depression over k, and the mole fraction built from that group carries no molecular weight, while k is 2335 on all four.", "Because the mole fraction is computed from the water side alone, using the water molecular weight of 18.015, so the inhibitor never enters it.", "Because the four concentrations are close enough that the mole fractions agree within the printing precision of the return."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At a 36.0 degF need the four designs are 33.0646363417, 48.9005409759, 62.0653353522 and 69.8364061014 weight percent, all on 0.21737265128002306.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 10, 'At a 36.0 degF need every one of the four fluids gives a Nielsen-Bucklin depression of 31.7647816499 degF. What does suppressing that number for the glycols cost?', '["The correct answer for the glycols, since Nielsen-Bucklin is only valid for methanol and would mislead on a glycol design.", "A tighter concentration, because a glycol design that saw the check would be resized upward and would then need a larger pump.", "The reliable flag, which has nothing to compare against on a glycol and therefore reports false at every concentration.", "A check and nothing else, because the number withheld from three of the fluids is the number already computed for the fourth."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Suppressing it removes a check and changes no answer, and a reader who sees a glycol design come back with a null check should not conclude it was validated some other way.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 11, 'The engine reports nielsenBucklinF as null for MEG at every concentration. Where does that number nonetheless exist?', '["In the committed golden case file, which publishes a Nielsen-Bucklin depression for all four fluids in all 24 rows.", "In the note attached to the return, which quotes it in prose for the fluids where the field itself is null.", "Nowhere. The relation is not defined for a glycol, which is why the catalogue flag was set false in the first place.", "In the harness only, which recomputes it to compare the two relations at the tighter relative tolerance of 1.0000e-9."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 50.0 weight percent MEG the golden reads 33.0254224092 degF against a Hammerschmidt value of 37.6123731271 degF, so the check the module declines to run sits in the file it is validated against.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 12, 'inhibitionRequirement is called with no subcoolingF at all. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming the missing input, in the same shape the injection rate uses for a missing water rate.", "ok true with a neededDepressionF of zero, so the design is sized for no depression and the rate comes back as zero barrels a day.", "ok true, required false, and a note saying the fluid sits outside the hydrate region by NaN F and no inhibitor is needed to keep it there.", "ok false with a NaN in neededDepressionF, which is the bare NaN policy the two depression helpers already follow."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The guard is !(need > 0), and that test is true for a NaN, so the branch written for a fluid already outside the region also catches a caller who said nothing.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 13, 'The swallowed trench, the swallowed cooldown mass and the missing subcooling are all the same NaN-is-falsy habit. What makes the last of the three the worst?', '["It is the only one of the three that reaches a user in prose, and a note reading NaN F is the sort of thing an operator ignores.", "The other two return a wrong number and this one returns a decision not to inhibit a line at all.", "It is the only one that cannot be reproduced, since the requirement chain is never exercised by the oracle or by any golden case.", "It is the only one where the wrong answer is larger than the right one, and an over-dose is harder to argue away than an under-dose."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A caller who omits the one input the whole chain is built on is told, with ok true, that no inhibitor is needed.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 14, 'depressionCheck is only a check under one condition. What is it?', '["That reliable comes back true, since a check computed above the reliability line carries no weight either way.", "That the fluid is methanol, because it is the only one of the four for which the module reports two relations at once.", "That the concentration sits below MAX_PRACTICAL_WT_PCT of 70.0 weight percent, above which the check is still computed but is no longer returned.", "That basis names a different relation from the one the dose was sized with, so two independent relations meet on one concentration."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Everywhere else the field is the sizing arithmetic printed twice, so reading it without reading basis is reading an echo.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-sized-one-way-checked-another', 15, 'A dose whose check agrees with its need to the last figure has proved what?', '["That the design is validated, because two independent routes to the same depression have landed on the same number.", "That the concentration sits below the practical ceiling, which is the only condition under which the two agree exactly.", "That an inverse inverted, since the same relation was run backwards and then forwards on one concentration.", "That the safety margin was large enough to absorb the disagreement between the two relations at that concentration."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A design that matches its own need to the last figure deserves the most suspicion rather than the least, and at a 20.0 degF need it does exactly that at 21.5337052221 weight percent.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 1, 'There is exactly one refusal branch in inhibitionRequirement below the rate. What does it compare?', '["recommendedF against neededDepressionF, which is why a design short of its subcooling comes back with an error rather than a rate.", "weightPct against maxWtPct, and nothing anywhere in the function compares the delivered depression against the need.", "The sized concentration against the reliability line of 25.0 weight percent, above which the design is refused as unvalidated.", "The mole fraction of the design against the mole fraction the ceiling implies, which is the coordinate both relations share."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The one column that would catch a short design is computed, returned in depressionCheck, and never read by anything.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 2, 'Sweeping methanol needs from 60.0 degF up, the shortfall runs from 10.6963872958 degF to 61.4038500495 degF at 170.0 degF. What does ok read across that sweep?', '["False from the row where the shortfall first exceeds the safety margin the design was given, which is what a sweep of this kind is built to locate.", "True until the delivered depression falls below the need, after which the function reports required false instead.", "True on every row, because the design is inside the concentration ceiling the whole way and shortfall is not a refusal condition.", "True on the rows below 100.0 degF of need and false above them, where the sized concentration crosses the reliability line for a second time."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The design at a 170.0 degF need is 69.9940887712 weight percent and delivers 108.5961499505 degF, and the return carries no note marking it short.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 3, 'The 180.0 degF row of that sweep is the only one refused. What is it refused for?', '["For a delivered depression of 112.8510939641 degF that falls further below its need than any other row in the table.", "For a concentration at which the aqueous phase is no longer water, which the function detects from the mole fraction of the design.", "For crossing both limits at once, since it is the first row where the shortfall and the concentration exceed their thresholds together.", "For asking 71.1806669793 weight percent, which is past the ceiling, rather than for being short by 67.1489060359 degF."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sort that table by shortfall and the refusal stays exactly where it is, because the two orderings are not the same ordering.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 4, 'At 70.0 weight percent methanol the engine''s two relations read 170.0478568456 degF and 108.6168490752 degF. What is the gap between them for?', '["It is the band the engine will design inside and the chemistry it checks against cannot deliver, 61.4310077704 degF wide.", "It is the uncertainty on the ceiling, which is why MAX_PRACTICAL_WT_PCT is an argument a user is allowed to move.", "It is the safety margin built into the ceiling, so a design at the limit still delivers on the relation the module prefers.", "It is a rounding artefact of the metric round trip, since the two relations are fitted to the same data in different variables."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ratio of the ceiling read in the two sets of coordinates is 1.5655753071, and the limit is drawn in the coordinates of the over-predicting relation.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 5, 'MEG at the same 70.0 weight percent ceiling gives a Hammerschmidt depression of 87.7772407497 degF. What does depression report as the cross check there?', '["87.7772407497 degF, the same number, since the catalogue makes the sizing relation the reported check for a glycol.", "108.6168490752 degF, because the check is computed on the mole fraction and the mole fraction does not know which fluid it came from.", "Null, as it does at every concentration for a glycol, even though running the other relation by hand gives 67.0209843317 degF.", "A refusal, since a ceiling reached on a fluid with no cross relation is the one case the module declines to report at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A glycol design at the ceiling therefore carries no accuracy-shaped signal at all beyond a reliable flag that is a print switch on concentration.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 6, 'injectionRate uses leanWtPct twice, one line apart. What are the two uses?', '["A mass fraction in the gross-up, then the same mass fraction in the reciprocal density blend, which is consistent throughout.", "A mass fraction in the gross-up of the pure inhibitor, then a volume fraction in the density blend on the next line.", "A volume fraction in the gross-up, then a mass fraction in the density blend, which is the wrong way round for both of them.", "A concentration in the water for the gross-up, then a strength in the umbilical for the density, which are two different streams."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mass fraction form is that the reciprocal of the density is the sum of each mass fraction over its own density, and only one of the two lines can be right.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 7, 'The engine returns a stream density of 6.6696000000 lb/gal on 96.00 weight percent lean methanol against a mass fraction blend of 6.6555426582. Which way does the rate then err, and can it ever go the other way?', '["Always high, since the volume rule overstates the volume the same pounds occupy and a rate is a volume per day.", "It depends on which component is the heavier one, so a methanol blend errs low and a MEG blend errs high.", "It depends on the lean strength, and the two rules cross at the 50.0 weight percent row of the sweep.", "Always low, because the arithmetic mean of two densities always exceeds the harmonic mean and the rate is a mass over that density."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The methanol rate comes back 0.211213 percent low at 307.7753251096 bbl/d against 308.4253852443, and the MEG rate 0.116326 percent low.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 8, 'The convention error in the density blend is a fraction of a percent on the teaching line streams. When does it stop being small?', '["As the lean stream is diluted: 0.495029 percent low at 90.0 weight percent lean methanol and 1.375082 percent at 50.0.", "When the produced water rate rises, since the error is proportional to the mass the balance has to gross up.", "When the target concentration in the water rises, because the two blends are evaluated at the design strength rather than the lean one.", "Never. It is bounded by the difference between the two catalogue densities and cannot exceed a fraction of a percent."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A recovered glycol stream comes back lean, so the error grows in exactly the case a real facility meets most often.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 9, 'Why does the density convention error matter more than its own size suggests?', '["It moves the concentration in the water, so the depression check is being run against a strength the line never reaches.", "It is the larger of the two errors in the requirement chain, once the shortfall is expressed as a fraction rather than in degF.", "It is an under-dose stacked on a dose already sized short, and two systematic errors pointing the same way do not cancel.", "It moves the stream density, which the profile then uses to form the pressure drop that carries the Joule-Thomson term."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The mass balance above it is right and neither 82766.408593 nor 86215.008951 lb per day moves, so the whole error is in the conversion to barrels.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 10, 'overallU returns referenceIdIn. Which of relaxationLengthFt, steadyStateProfile and cooldownTime reads it?', '["All three of them, which is why a coefficient paired with the wrong diameter raises a written complaint rather than returning a number.", "None of the three. Each takes a bare diameter, so pairing the coefficient with its own reference is the caller''s job.", "Only steadyStateProfile, since it is the one consumer of the three that builds an area rather than a length out of the diameter.", "Only cooldownTime, which needs the area to form U A per foot and therefore has to know which diameter was named."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field is reported and no consumer accepts it, and nothing complains when the caller separates the two.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 11, 'The published buried build reads 0.713200037662 Btu/(hr ft2 degF) on the bore and 0.501513997498 on the coated outside diameter. What is invariant between them?', '["The coefficient itself once the areas are divided out, which is why the two numbers agree to 4.4409e-16 relative.", "The ratio of the two coefficients, 1.4220939819, which is a property of the stack rather than of the diameters chosen.", "U divided by its own reference diameter, which is the conductance per unit area and is what a heat transfer coefficient means.", "U times its own reference diameter, 0.360463185702 Btu/(hr ft degF) per foot of pipe either way, and pi times that is one over the total resistance."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both builds carry a total resistance of 0.883057962117 hr ft degF/Btu per foot, identical to the last figure, because the physics did not move.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 12, 'An outside-diameter-referred U of 0.5015139975 is handed to relaxationLengthFt with the 6.065 in bore. What happens?', '["The call is refused, because the module checks the diameter it was given against the referenceIdIn on the coefficient.", "The call succeeds and the error cancels in ntu, since the same diameter appears in the length and in the area.", "The call succeeds with ok true and returns 75347.484814 ft against the correct 52983.477727, wrong by 42.209398 percent.", "The call succeeds and the relaxation length is wrong by 1.4220939819 ft, which is the ratio of the two reference diameters."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That percentage is exactly the reference ratio of 8.625 over 6.065 and nothing else, and the mix is dimensionally consistent so nothing complains.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 13, 'The mixed reference is one error in ntu. Why is it worth 3.80418823 degF in one place and 15.39298092 degF in another?', '["Because ntu sits inside an exponential, so where the line already is on its own curve decides what a fixed error in ntu is worth in degF.", "Because the error in relaxation length grows with the length of the line, from a few percent at 5280.0 ft to more than forty at 105600.0 ft.", "Because the arrival at 5280.0 ft is still near the inlet, where the exponential is flat and a temperature error cannot yet develop.", "Because the two lengths carry different U values, so the reference mismatch is worth more on the longer of the two stacks."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The relaxation length is 42.209398 percent wrong in every one of those cases, and only the arrival error changes size.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 14, 'The module carries a Hammerschmidt constant of 2335.000000 and a Nielsen-Bucklin constant of 129.600000. Why are those two not independent?', '["Because the oracle reaches both through the same metric round trip, so any change to one moves the other by the same ratio.", "For the module''s own two relations to meet as the aqueous phase goes dilute, the first has to be 129.6 times 18.015, which is 2334.744000.", "Because Nielsen-Bucklin is the dilute limit of Hammerschmidt, so the two constants are the same number in different units.", "Because the harness compares them against each other, and a constant that failed that comparison could not have been committed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oracle''s metric round trip reaches a third value, 2334.600000, so one quantity has three values and the carried one matches neither.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-ceilings-and-conventions', 15, 'Every published Hammerschmidt row differs from its golden by 1.713062e-4, on all four fluids and all six concentrations. Why did that never fail the harness?', '["Because a relative difference identical on every row is treated as a round trip rather than as a disagreement, and is excluded.", "Because the harness compares the engine against the engine on those rows, and reaches the golden only through Nielsen-Bucklin.", "The Hammerschmidt tolerance is 5.0000e-4 and the actual gap between the carried and the metric constants is 1.713356e-4, so it fits inside.", "Because the tolerance is applied to the depression in Celsius, where the same absolute gap is 1.8 times smaller."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nielsen-Bucklin is compared at 1.0000e-9, a ratio of 500000.0 between the two tolerances, and the gap would not have survived the tighter one.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'A flow assurance verdict has a temperature half and a chemistry half. What do the two have in common?', '["Both are validated against the independent SI oracle at every published condition, so a disagreement between them can only ever be a difference of convention rather than of physics.", "Both are computed from the hydrate boundary the laboratory supplied, which is why one measured number decides the whole answer at either end of the study and nothing else does.", "Neither of them fails: both return ok true with no note, and in both the evidence that the answer is wrong is a field of the same object, printed and unread.", "Both refuse cleanly at their own limits, which is why a study that reaches an answer at all can be taken as sound."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The temperature half moves 89.316029952695 degF to 64.1160299527 degF across a 71.00 degF boundary, and the chemistry half sizes 36.0035520084 weight percent and checks it at 35.6195882812 degF.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'Which quantities in a flow assurance verdict does neither engine compute?', '["The hydrate boundary in both its states, flowing and packed up after a shutdown, which is a laboratory or flash result the consumer supplies.", "The overall coefficient and the relaxation length, which are properties of a pipe and of a chosen area rather than of the modules that consume them downstream.", "The subcooling and the depression, both of which arrive as inputs and neither of which any function in the course returns.", "The arrival temperature, which is read off the station table rather than computed, and the no-touch time that follows from it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both module headers say so in as many words, so every margin and every subcooling in this course rests on a number somebody else measured.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'MAX_PRACTICAL_WT_PCT is 70.0 weight percent, and at that concentration the two relations read 170.0478568456 degF and 108.6168490752 degF. What does the pattern of the tier look like in that one fact?', '["A limit is being enforced twice, once on each relation, which is why the accepted band between them is so wide.", "A limit is being enforced, and it is being enforced in the coordinates of the relation the module itself says over-predicts up there.", "A limit is not being enforced at all, since a design is accepted at any concentration and only the reported depression changes.", "A limit is being enforced correctly, and the gap between the two readings is the uncertainty a designer is expected to carry."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same shape runs through the whole tier: two numbers that belong to each other are computed and never set against each other.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'A study is worked in the order the module recommends. What comes first, before any pipe is built?', '["Building the resistance stack, since the overall coefficient is the quantity every later number in the module is derived from and nothing can be checked before it exists.", "Choosing the reference diameter, because a coefficient with no named area is not a number any consumer can use and the choice cannot be undone later.", "Sizing the inhibitor dose, since the chemistry sets the arrival temperature the thermal half then has to reach.", "Writing down the flowing and the shut-in hydrate boundary with a source against each, because every later margin is conditional on them."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A line packs up after a shutdown, so the shut-in boundary is the higher of the two and it is the one a no-touch time is measured against.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'Which step, taken out of order, leaves every later number defensible and the verdict wrong?', '["Counting the resistance terms, since a dropped ground term is worth 79.035960 percent on the coefficient and everything after it.", "Pairing the reference diameter with the coefficient, since a mixed pair is dimensionally consistent and raises no complaint anywhere.", "Establishing the hydrate boundary, because every later quantity is computed correctly and compared against the wrong line.", "Running the heat loss profile before the pressures are passed, since the two arrivals cannot be separated once both are applied."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each function answers exactly the question it was asked, and a verdict is a comparison against an input rather than a computed result.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'overallU returns a resistances array. What should a reviewer count in it, and against what?', '["The number of entries against the number of layers alone, since the films are always present and never go missing.", "Two films, one entry per layer, and one burial entry if a trench was asked for, against what was actually handed in.", "The shares in each entry, which have to sum to 100 percent, and any shortfall is the term that was dropped.", "The total resistance against the coefficient, since the product of the two is fixed and a missing term breaks it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the published 8.625 in coated diameter the trench floor is 0.35937500 ft, and counting terms is the only check the API offers against a trench below it.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'How is a returned coefficient checked against the reference diameter it was quoted on?', '["By the ratio of the two coefficients, which has to match the ratio of the two reference diameters exactly on any build of the same pipe.", "By the total resistance, which is the field both references share and which changes only when the pipe changes.", "By referenceIdIn itself, which each consumer reads and compares against the diameter it was handed.", "By the product: U times its own reference diameter is the same either way, and pi times that is one over the total resistance."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The three consumers take a bare diameter and cannot see the field, so keeping the pair together is the caller''s job and nothing complains when it is not.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'A gas line profile is being reported. What does the module''s own recommended practice say to write down?', '["Three arrivals, the heat loss one, the engine one and the correctly damped one, with the damping factor beside them.", "The engine arrival alone, with the pressure column attached, since it is the only one the shipped code returns.", "The heat loss arrival and the engine arrival, whose difference is the Joule-Thomson term and needs no further correction.", "The engine arrival and the ntu, from which any reader can recover whichever of the other two they want."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Three arrivals with the factor beside them is an answer somebody can check, and one arrival is a number.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'Before reading the hours out of a cooldown, what has to be checked, and why will the function not check it?', '["The mass slots against the layer list, because the function has two slots and the stack may have more layers than that.", "The U against the bore film, because a shut-in line has a stagnant bore and the coefficient handed in was measured flowing.", "The start against the target, because cooldownTime guards both temperatures against ambient and never guards one against the other.", "The ambient against the seabed, because the branch that returns Infinity fires only when the target is below ambient."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On an inverted pair it returns -4.6959175559 hr with ok true and a station table that warms by 24.2422513458 degF after a shutdown.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'A glycol design comes back with depressionCheck.nielsenBucklinF reading null. What has that established?', '["That the two relations agreed, since a spread of null means there was no spread worth reporting on that row.", "Nothing was checked. A null is an absence of a check rather than an absence of disagreement between the relations.", "That the design sits below the reliability line, which is the condition under which the module reports one relation only.", "That the check was run and returned the sizing relation, which for a glycol is the only relation the catalogue makes available."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The number withheld is one the module already computes for methanol at the same mole fraction, so nothing was validated some other way.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'What do these two engines genuinely earn?', '["A hydrate verdict, since the arrival and the boundary are both returned by the same module and compared inside it.", "A validated pressure traverse, which is what makes the Joule-Thomson term reportable on a published case.", "A cooldown that carries the whole line, since the two mass slots between them cover every layer in the stack.", "A resistance stack that adds in one direction with every share readable, and from it a length, an arrival, an inverse, a cooldown and a dose."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the published buried build the foam carries 52.83080440 percent and the trench 46.57193819 percent, and that same foam layer carries 98.88212788 percent once the trench is taken away, on an unchanged resistance of 0.4665266247 hr ft degF/Btu per foot.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'The conductivity helper is handed an insulation id it does not recognise. What does it return, and why was it written that way?', '["A NaN, because an earlier version returned carbon steel for an unknown id and the two conductivities differ by 2166.666667.", "Carbon steel, as the safe default, since a stack that omits a layer is worse than one that models it as the strongest material.", "A refusal object naming the id, which is the same shape overallU uses when a layer cannot be resolved from its geometry.", "The nearest catalogue entry by name, which is why a typo in an insulation id is caught before it reaches the stack."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is one of the places the module refuses well, and a typo in an insulation id would otherwise make a line look two thousand times better insulated than it is.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'The burial term is a shape factor for an isothermal cylinder in a semi-infinite medium. Which of its assumptions is easiest to break by accident?', '["That the soil conductivity is uniform everywhere around the pipe, since a seabed layered between wet soil and dry soil is the ordinary case rather than the exception.", "That the surface above the trench sits at ambient, which fails on any line whose burial is shallower than the seasonal thermal front the seabed carries with it through the year.", "That the burial is measured to the centreline, since reading the same trench to the top of pipe moves the ground term by 2.784925 percent with nothing to catch it.", "That there is no groundwater movement, which no subsea trench satisfies and which the module has no input to describe."]'::jsonb, 2, 'None of those assumptions is checked by anything in the module, and the centreline convention is the one a survey can silently disagree with.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'What is the Expert tier''s claim about these two engines?', '["That the published cases are too narrow to validate the functions a design actually uses, so the goldens prove very little.", "Not that either is unreliable, but that the seams between correct answers are where a study goes wrong.", "That the two modules disagree with each other, which is why a verdict built from both of them cannot be defended.", "That the correlations are dated, and that a modern flash would replace both the depression relations and the shape factor."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every published quantity agrees with an independent oracle, and the resistance stack, the relaxation length, the arrival and the depression relations are all sound.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'Two returns in these modules carry ok true on an answer nobody should use. What makes them a pair rather than two separate defects?', '["Each is caught by the harness at a relative tolerance the goldens are compared at, and neither survives a tighter one.", "Each fails only on a teaching line, since no published case exercises either the pressure inputs or the requirement chain.", "Each returns a number that is wrong in the safe direction, so a design built on either is expensive rather than dangerous.", "Each answers the question it was asked correctly, and the question a design needed answered was one field further on."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reading a returned object as a conclusion is the mistake, and both the trench dropped from a stack and the check never compared pass every test the return supports.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'A study subtracts the shipped engine arrival of 64.116029952695 degF from the 71.00 degF flowing boundary and sizes an inhibitor on the difference. What has it inherited before any chemistry is questioned?', '["Nothing, since the arrival and the boundary are both returned by modules that were validated against the same independent oracle.", "A subcooling built on an arrival 10.6383092062 degF colder than the damped balance gives, so the dose is sized for a risk the correctly weighted profile does not show.", "A subcooling that is too small, because the undamped Joule-Thomson term moves the arrival away from the boundary rather than across it.", "An arrival that cannot be used at all, because a profile carrying pressures returns a station table with no margin column in it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correctly damped arrival is 74.754339158867 degF, which sits outside the same boundary, so the two readings do not even agree on whether a dose is needed.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'A trench is entered below its D/2 floor and the resulting stack is carried all the way through to an inhibitor dose. Which way does the chemistry move?', '["Downward, since a higher U shortens the relaxation length and a shorter relaxation length leaves the fluid warmer at the far end.", "Not at all, because the requirement chain takes a subcooling as an input and never sees the thermal stack that produced it.", "It depends on the fluid, since a gas line and a liquid line move in opposite directions when the coefficient is overstated.", "Upward. The dropped term raises U, cools the computed arrival, deepens the subcooling and buys a larger dose than the line needs."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A dropped resistance can only ever raise U, so the whole chain past it errs in one direction: 79.035960 percent on the published pipe and an over-dose at the end of it.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'steadyStateProfile returns 43.8877170663 degF on a line losing heat to a 45.00 degF seabed, and cooldownTime returns -4.6959175559 hr on an inverted temperature pair. What do the two cases share beyond ok true?', '["In each case another function in the same file refuses the very pair the first one answered.", "In each case the returned object carries a note that a careful caller could read, which is what separates them from a silent failure.", "In each case the error is bounded by the damping factor of 1.730568266886, which is what makes both of them recoverable after the fact.", "In each case the published goldens cover the condition, so the harness would have caught either one before it shipped."]'::jsonb, 0, 'uForArrivalTemp refuses a target at or below ambient and refuses an inlet below its target, and neither refusal knows the forward function was asked the same thing.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'One constant of 25.0 weight percent does two separate jobs across the inhibitor modules. What are they?', '["It sets the reliability of the Hammerschmidt relation and it caps the concentration a design is allowed to reach.", "It marks where the two relations begin to disagree and it marks where the injection rate switches to the mass fraction density blend.", "It decides which relation is printed as recommendedF, and it is therefore the concentration at which a sized dose stops agreeing with its own check.", "It fixes the mole fraction the inverse solves to and it fixes the basis the golden case file was cut against."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Below it the check runs the sizing relation forward and restates its input, and above it the check runs a different relation and a shortfall appears in one row.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'A need of 170.0 degF is accepted at 69.9940887712 weight percent and a need of 180.0 degF is refused at 71.1806669793. Which return is the more dangerous to receive?', '["The refused one, since a refusal quoted on a concentration hides the real problem and sends the designer away to look at the wrong quantity entirely.", "The accepted one, because a refusal at least stops a design while an acceptance short by 61.4038500495 degF carries on into a rate.", "Neither, because both rows sit above what the module''s preferred relation can deliver and a reader will notice either way.", "The refused one, because it is the only row of the sweep whose depressionCheck is not returned at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The refusal fires when a concentration crosses a line and never when a depression does, so the worst answer in the table is the last one accepted.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'The swallowed trench and the missing subcoolingF are the same NaN-is-falsy habit. What is different about what each tells the caller?', '["One returns a note and the other returns none, so only the first can be caught from the object that came back.", "One is refused when both of its inputs are bad and the other is refused when either of its inputs is bad.", "One is exercised by the published goldens and the other is not, so only the first was ever compared against the oracle.", "One returns a coefficient that is wrong and the other returns a decision that no inhibitor is needed at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The note on the second prints the words NaN F into a sentence meant for a user, and ok reads true beside required false.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'A profile is given an inlet pressure and no outlet, and the pressure column comes back flat at 2400.0 psia. Which earlier failure is that the same shape as?', '["The swallowed trench, because in both a value that could not be computed is replaced by a plausible one instead of by an absence.", "The inverted cooldown, because in both the returned table runs in a direction the physics does not allow.", "The suppressed Nielsen-Bucklin check, because in both a field is set to null and a reader takes the null for agreement.", "The mixed reference diameter, because in both the caller supplied two things that were individually valid and did not belong together."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A column of n/a is how a reader knows the term was not applied, and a half specified input replaces that tell with a pressure the line never had.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Some quantities in these modules travel between two returns and some do not. Which is which?', '["The overall coefficient and the total resistance travel, while the relaxation length and the ntu do not.", "The depression travels, since both relations return it in degF, while the concentration does not because its basis changes.", "The mole fraction and the conductance per foot travel, while referenceIdIn and leanWtPct do not.", "Nothing travels. Every field in these modules is meaningful only inside the function that produced it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Methanol and MEG sized for one need both land on 0.2403082363926517, and U times its own reference diameter is 0.360463185702 Btu/(hr ft degF) per foot on either reference.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Two of these findings are worth several degF on the teaching line: the undamped Joule-Thomson term and the never compared depression check. What do they have in common?', '["Each is a unit error, one in degF per psi and one in weight percent, and both survive because the arithmetic is dimensionally sound.", "Each is caught by the published goldens at the looser of the two harness tolerances, and neither survives the tighter one.", "Each moves the answer in the conservative direction, so both cost money and neither costs safety.", "Each is a comparison the module had all the numbers for and never made."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The profile returns the ntu the damping is built from, and the requirement returns the delivered depression, and neither function reads its own output back.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'The published profiles arrive at 156.177943871283 degF, 95.094251524883 degF and 43.357693442744 degF. What are those three?', '["Heat loss and nothing else, because no golden sets a pressure and the term is exactly zero in all of them.", "Heat loss with the catalogue Joule-Thomson coefficient applied, which is why they fall faster than the exponential alone.", "Three lengths of one line with the term applied undamped, which is the case the oracle was cut to check.", "Three separate builds at one length, distinguished by the coefficient each was given."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pressure column on the longest of them reads n/a at every station rather than hiding the absence.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'Push the teaching line to 210000.0 ft. The heat loss column reads 47.1024942545 degF and the damped column 41.2801820293 degF. Where does the engine column sit?', '["At 41.2801820293 degF, since at a large ntu the damping factor and the undamped term converge on one and the same answer at the far end.", "At 47.1024942545 degF, since past an ntu of about four the exponential has run out and the two columns meet.", "At 21.9024942545 degF, well below the 45.00 degF seabed, because the term subtracted does not shrink as the line lengthens.", "Below the damped column by 25.2000000000 degF, which is the whole term applied to a line that has already relaxed toward the seabed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The damped column stays above the seabed at every length in the sweep and the engine column crosses it between 60000.0 ft and 90000.0 ft.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'On the teaching line the inlet excess over the seabed is 150.0000 degF and exp(-ntu) is 0.295440199685. What survives to the far end?', '["18.3160299527 degF of excess, which is the margin the arrival keeps against the flowing hydrate boundary.", "44.3160299527 degF of excess, which is the arrival of 89.316029952695 degF against a 45.00 degF seabed.", "150.0000 degF times the damping factor of 1.730568266886, because the excess and the term decay through the same weighting.", "Nothing measurable, since an ntu above one leaves less than a third of the excess and the rest is inside the noise."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That product is the whole heat loss answer, and it is the row the three ways of applying the Joule-Thomson term are measured from.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'uForArrivalTemp on the teaching line says the 71.00 degF boundary needs a U of 0.651078288819 Btu/(hr ft2 degF) against the 0.452972856617 the line has. What is that ratio of 1.43734504 saying?', '["That the line needs 1.43734504 times more insulation than it has to clear the boundary with the pressures applied.", "That the inverse and the forward profile disagree by that factor, which is precisely the damping the forward profile leaves out.", "That the boundary sits 1.43734504 times further from the seabed than the arrival does, measured in units of the inlet excess.", "That the line would have to lose heat far faster than it does before its heat loss arrival reached the boundary at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The inverse agrees with the heat loss row: the U a target needs is above the U the line has, which is the same statement as the arrival being above the target.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'With heat loss alone, at which of the 21 teaching line stations does the fluid first fall below the 71.00 degF flowing boundary?', '["At station 20, the arrival, where the margin has fallen to 18.3160299527 degF and crosses on the last increment.", "At station 16 and every station after it, once the temperature has fallen to 101.5544690683 degF.", "At none of them. The coldest point is the arrival at 89.31602995 degF, which is still above the boundary.", "It cannot be read from the table, because only the even stations are printed and the crossing falls between two of them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Station 20 at 60000.00 ft is the last of the 21 and the whole profile falls monotonically toward the seabed, so the arrival is the coldest point on it.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'For natural gas at flowline conditions the Joule-Thomson coefficient carries a sign. Which, and what follows from it?', '["Negative, so expansion warms the fluid, which is why the term has to be subtracted rather than added.", "Positive, so expansion cools and the term subtracts from the arrival the heat loss balance would otherwise give.", "Positive for a gas and negative for a liquid, which is why the module carries the coefficient as an input rather than a catalogue value.", "It has no sign, because the pressure drop already carries the direction and the coefficient is a magnitude."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The coefficient comes out of an equation of state and the pressure traverse is a hydraulics problem, and this module solves neither of them.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'A pipe lying on the seabed has H equal to D on 2, and the ground resistance there comes back as 0.0000000000 hr ft degF/Btu per foot. Is that a limitation?', '["Yes, because the shape factor cannot represent a pipe in contact with the seabed and returns a floor instead.", "Yes, because the golden publishes 1.6148921380e-9 K m / W for the same case and the two do not agree.", "No, but only because the soil conductivity was 1.2 Btu/(hr ft degF): a lower conductivity leaves a residue at the same depth.", "No. A pipe lying on the bottom adds no ground resistance, and acosh vanishing there is the check that the shape factor is the right one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both the SI residue and the field one are floating point remains of an exact zero, reached by different routes, and neither is a disagreement about physics.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'The published depth sweep gives 0.0624838383 hr ft degF/Btu per foot at 0.400000 ft and nothing at all just below it. What sits between those two rows?', '["The refusal boundary, where the module stops accepting a trench and returns a written reason instead.", "The point at which the ground share reaches 11.694861 percent, below which the term is dropped as negligible.", "The floor at 0.35937500 ft, where 2H/D reaches 1 and the term goes from a small number to a NaN.", "Nothing. The term falls smoothly to zero as the burial approaches the pipe lying on the bottom."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A trench typed as 0.3 ft rather than 3.0 ft falls under that floor, and the term is then caught by Number.isFinite and never pushed.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'overallU refuses a layer with an unresolvable conductivity and accepts a trench it could not compute. What does the module''s own header say about the difference?', '["That geometry is checked and ground conditions are not, because a trench is a site condition rather than a property of the pipe.", "That a NaN propagates into a refusal and a plausible wrong number does not, which is the discipline the trench branch does not follow.", "That a term with a share below a threshold may be dropped, which is why the ground term is treated differently from a layer.", "That the resistances array records what was used, so a dropped term needs no refusal because it is already visible."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One input class, two opposite treatments in one function, and the swallowed one comes back as a plausible wrong number.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'cooldownTime with both mass slots NaN is refused and with one slot NaN is not. What decides that?', '["The refusal is written on the contents slot alone, since a line with no contents has nothing to cool.", "The refusal is written on the U rather than on the two masses, and one good mass slot still gives a finite time constant to divide by.", "The refusal is written on the station count, and one good mass still produces the full table of 25 stations.", "The guard is written on the total heat capacity, so it fires only when the sum reaches zero rather than when a slot does."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both slots read as a mass or zero and NaN is falsy, so one bad slot returns 1.2806433091 hr with ok true and no note against a correct 4.6627248553 hr.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'A cooldown whose target sits below ambient returns hours of Infinity with ok true. Why is that a good return?', '["It refuses in the only way a limit can be refused, by returning a value no caller can mistake for an answer.", "It carries a meaningful time constant, an empty station list and a written note saying the line settles at ambient and never reaches the target.", "It matches the shape uForArrivalTemp uses for a target below ambient, so the two functions agree on the same case.", "It is bounded by the time constant, so a caller reading hours alone still gets a number in the right range."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is the shape the missing branch should have had, and the same function answers an inverted pair with a negative number and no note at all.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'What does a dropped ground term do to the ntu of a line, and why does the size of that matter?', '["It raises ntu by the same factor it raises U, and because ntu sits in an exponent the temperature error depends on where the line already is on its own curve.", "It leaves ntu unchanged, since the length and the relaxation length move together when the coefficient moves.", "It lowers ntu, because a shorter relaxation length means fewer transfer units over the same length of pipe.", "It raises ntu in proportion to the share the ground term carried, which is 44.145299 percent on the published pipe."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A sweep on a 180.0 degF inlet against a 40.0 degF ambient arrives at 124.9142923598 degF at an ntu of 0.5000 and 58.9469396531 degF at an ntu of 2.0000.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'The teaching line has two boundaries, 71.00 degF flowing and 78.00 degF packed up, and two arrivals to compare them against. Which subcooling belongs in a dose calculation?', '["The one built from the flowing boundary and the flowing arrival, since that is the condition the line spends its life in.", "The larger of the four, whichever it turns out to be, because a dose sized on the worst case covers the others.", "The one built from the flowing boundary and the shut-in temperature, since the boundary is a fluid property and does not move.", "The one built from the packed up boundary and the temperature the line sits at once it has stopped."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The boundary moves out when the line packs up and the fluid is colder at the same time, so the error from taking the flowing pair compounds from both ends.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'All 24 published inhibitor rows differ from their goldens by the same 1.713062e-4 on Hammerschmidt. What does one identical relative difference across four fluids and six concentrations mean?', '["That the engine and the oracle use different molecular weights, whose ratio is fixed and cancels the concentration out.", "That the harness rounds every row to the same precision before comparing, so the difference is an artefact of the report.", "That it is one constant against another and nothing else, since anything depending on the fluid or the concentration would vary down the column.", "That the two implementations agree exactly and the residue is the floating point cost of the metric round trip."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Nielsen-Bucklin rows agree at the round trip instead, so the two implementations disagree on one relation by a fixed ratio and agree on the other.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'TEG appears in the inhibitor catalogue at a molecular weight of 150.17 and a density of 9.4000 lb/gal. Why is it there?', '["Because it is the strongest of the four per pound, which is what the molecular weight ordering shows.", "For completeness. It is a dehydration fluid rather than an injection inhibitor, and at 20.0 weight percent it gives 3.8872611041 degF.", "Because it is the only one of the four with a Nielsen-Bucklin check available, so it anchors the other three.", "Because a dehydration fluid and an injection inhibitor are the same duty seen from two ends of a facility."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The depression goes inversely with molecular weight at a fixed weight percent, so the heaviest fluid in the catalogue is the weakest inhibitor in it.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'The ceiling refusal quotes the concentration it reached to one decimal place. Why one rather than none?', '["At whole percent a concentration just over the limit prints as the limit itself, and the sentence would then name a ceiling the number appears to satisfy.", "Because the inverse is only accurate to one decimal at that end of its range, and quoting more would overstate it.", "Because the ceiling is an argument a user can move, and a decimal place lets them see how far past it they went.", "Because the golden case file publishes concentrations to one decimal, and the message quotes the same precision."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is the difference between a message that explains a refusal and a message that looks like a mistake.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'inhibitionRequirement has three ways out before an injection rate is ever computed. What are they?', '["A missing water rate, a missing lean strength, and a lean stream weaker than the concentration it has to produce.", "A subcooling below zero, a depression check that falls short of the need, and a concentration above the reliability line.", "A fluid with no Nielsen-Bucklin flag, a molecular weight that is not positive, and a hydrate boundary that was never supplied.", "A need that is not positive, a concentration outside 0 to 100 weight percent, and a concentration above the practical ceiling."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Only the ceiling refusal names a way out, saying the problem is thermal or one of dosing strategy rather than one of concentration.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'injectionRate returns rateBpd, rateGpd, massLbDay, pureMassLbDay and streamDensityLbGal. Which pair tells a facility what to build?', '["rateBpd against rateGpd, because they are the same quantity in the two units a facility works in.", "streamDensityLbGal against the catalogue inhibitor density, whose ratio is the dilution the lean stream carries.", "massLbDay against pureMassLbDay, because the line carries the lean stream and the pump and umbilical are sized on the larger figure.", "rateBpd against the produced water rate, whose ratio is the concentration the design was sized to reach."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A design quoting 82766.408593 lb/day rather than 86215.008951 lb/day understates both the pump and the umbilical.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'HAMMERSCHMIDT_RELIABLE_WT_PCT and MAX_PRACTICAL_WT_PCT are both exported and the ceiling is an argument with a default. What follows?', '["They are validated constants, since every one of the published rows was cut against them and the harness compares each of them in turn.", "They are conventions rather than physics, so a design quoting one is quoting the module''s default and not a limit of nature.", "They are safety factors, so a design that respects both of them has a documented margin against the relation each one guards.", "They are fluid properties, which is why the module carries a separate Hammerschmidt constant on each inhibitor."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One of the two is a print switch on concentration and the other is a refusal boundary drawn in the coordinates of the relation the module says over-predicts.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'At a need of 30.0 degF the requirement sizes 29.1608518901 weight percent and its own check reports 26.9823691235 degF. Why is a shortfall of 3.0176308765 degF not a small number?', '["Because it is a shortfall against a need that already contains the whole safety margin, and a hydrate verdict is decided on the margin.", "Because 10.058770 percent is a large fraction of any depression, and percentages of that size are never acceptable in a design of this kind.", "Because it grows without limit as the need rises, so a design at any deeper subcooling is short by more than the whole of the need itself.", "Because the check was run at a different concentration from the one the dose was sized at, so the two are not comparable."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Percentages of a depression are not percentages of a margin, and the margin is the whole quantity a dose was bought to protect.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'At 30.0 weight percent methanol the engine reports recommendedF as 27.9798914770 degF rather than 31.2332798288 degF. What did that change?', '["The relation the sizing used, which switches to Nielsen-Bucklin above the reliability line and returns a higher concentration.", "The reliability of the answer, since the smaller of the two relations is the one the module has validated at that strength.", "Which number is printed, and nothing about the concentration anybody chose or the dose anybody will inject.", "The basis of the golden comparison, since the harness compares recommendedF against the published value on each row."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The flip is a report and not a redesign, and it is exactly where a check that had been restating its own input starts running a second relation.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Sized to a 36.0 degF need, methanol lands at 33.0646363417 weight percent and TEG at 69.8364061014. What do the two designs have in common?', '["One injection rate, since the heavier fluid at a higher concentration carries the same pounds of inhibitor per day.", "One mole fraction, 0.21737265128002306 in both, and therefore one Nielsen-Bucklin depression of 31.7647816499 degF.", "One Hammerschmidt constant and one density, which is what makes the two concentrations differ by molecular weight alone.", "Nothing beyond the need they were sized to, since the two fluids differ in molecular weight by more than a factor of four."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The inverse fixes a group containing no molecular weight and all four fluids carry the same k of 2335, so one need gives one mole fraction.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'A reader sees a MEG design return recommendedF equal to its need exactly and nielsenBucklinF null. What conclusion is available?', '["That the design was validated by the golden, which publishes a Nielsen-Bucklin depression for MEG at every concentration.", "That the design agrees with itself, which is the strongest statement a single relation can support.", "That the design sits below the reliability line, since basis stays hammerschmidt only under that condition.", "Only that a second relation was not run, which is weaker than either agreement or disagreement."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 50.0 weight percent MEG the committed golden reads 33.0254224092 degF against a Hammerschmidt value of 37.6123731271 degF, so the check exists in the file the engine is validated against.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'The requirement is called with no subcoolingF and the guard is !(need > 0). Why does that guard catch the call?', '["Because a NaN fails every comparison, so the test that a need is not positive is true for a need nobody supplied.", "Because a missing input defaults to zero, and zero is not positive, so the branch fires on the default rather than on the NaN.", "Because the safety margin is added first and a margin added to nothing is still a number, which then fails the positivity test.", "Because the guard is written on the concentration rather than on the need, and no concentration is produced when the need is missing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The branch was written for a fluid already outside the hydrate region, and it also catches a caller who said nothing about where the fluid is.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'The requirement accepts needs of 120.0, 140.0 and 160.0 degF for methanol. What can 70.0 weight percent methanol actually kill on the relation the module prefers?', '["170.0478568456 degF, which is why the three rows are accepted and the ceiling is doing its job.", "108.6168490752 degF, so all three of those accepted rows ask for more than the ceiling itself can deliver.", "61.4310077704 degF, which is the width of the band between the two relations at the ceiling.", "It cannot be said, because the module reports no depression at all once a concentration reaches the ceiling."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The deepest accepted row asks for 170.0 degF and designs 69.9940887712 weight percent, so the last acceptance sits far past what that concentration can do.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'MAX_PRACTICAL_WT_PCT exists because the Hammerschmidt inverse is asymptotic to 100 weight percent. Is that a good reason for a limit?', '["No, because the inverse never breaks and a ceiling on a well behaved function is an arbitrary restriction.", "Yes, and measuring it in Hammerschmidt coordinates is correct, since that is the relation the concentration came from.", "No, because the real limit is the reliability line at 25.0 weight percent and a second ceiling adds nothing.", "Yes, and it is not a reason to measure the limit with the relation that over-predicts at that end of the range."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Past roughly that concentration the aqueous phase is barely water, and deep subcooling is a thermal problem rather than one of concentration.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'At 50.0 weight percent lean methanol the engine blends 7.4700000000 lb/gal and the mass fraction rule gives 7.3686746988. What does that do to the rate?', '["It leaves it 1.375082 percent high, because a lower stream density means that the same pounds a day occupy more gallons a day.", "Nothing at all, because the gross-up on the mass and the blend on the density use the same lean strength and the two of them cancel.", "It leaves it 1.375082 percent low, because the rate is a mass over a density and the engine density is the higher of the two.", "It leaves it low by the difference between the two blended densities, expressed as a fraction of the rate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The arithmetic mean of two densities always exceeds the harmonic mean whichever component is heavier, so the error has one sign at every lean strength.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'An engineer picks the outside-diameter-referred coefficient because a coated diameter feels like the honest outside of a line, then hands it on with the bore. What happens at 105600.0 ft?', '["The arrival reads 74.47159964 degF against a correct 59.07861872, on an ntu of 1.40150664 against 1.99307415.", "The arrival is unchanged, since both the coefficient and the diameter appear in ntu and the choice cancels between them.", "The call is refused, because the module compares the diameter it was handed against the reference the coefficient carries.", "The arrival reads 13.55773419 degF too warm, which is the fixed error the mixed pair carries at any length."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The relaxation length is 42.209398 percent wrong in every case and only the temperature error changes size, from 3.80418823 degF at 5280.0 ft upward.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'A reviewer is handed a coefficient of 0.501513997498 Btu/(hr ft2 degF) and told nothing else. What is missing?', '["The total resistance, since the coefficient alone cannot be turned back into the stack that produced it.", "The layer list, since two different builds can produce the same coefficient and the shares are what distinguish them.", "The mass rate and the heat capacity, without which the coefficient cannot be turned into a relaxation length.", "The area it is referred to, because a coefficient is a property of the pipe and a chosen area together."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same physical line reads 0.713200037662 on the bore and 0.501513997498 on the coated outside diameter, on one total resistance of 0.883057962117.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'One quantity in the inhibitor module has three values: 2335.000000, 2334.744000 and 2334.600000. What is each?', '["The constant for methanol, the constant for the glycols, and the constant the harness compares both against.", "The constant the module carries, the constant that makes its own two relations meet in the dilute limit, and the constant the oracle reaches through its metric round trip.", "The value at the reliability line, the value at the practical ceiling, and the value in the dilute limit.", "The published golden, the shipped engine value, and the value the teaching line was constructed to use."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Carried against meeting is 1.0001096480 and carried against metric is 1.0001713356, so the module is using two of the three at once.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'A flow assurance study is being handed over. Which four things is it entitled to claim it established?', '["A hydrate boundary, a subcooling, an arrival and a margin, which between them are the whole verdict.", "A pressure traverse, a Joule-Thomson term, an arrival and a no-touch time, which is what a gas line needs.", "A resistance stack with readable shares, a relaxation length and arrival from it, an inverse for a target, and a dose with a rate under it.", "A coefficient, a mass, a time constant and a station table, which is everything the cooldown function returns."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every one of those is validated against an independent oracle on the published cases, and none of them is the boundary the verdict is measured against.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'The pressure profile inside steadyStateProfile is an assumption as well as an input. What is assumed about it?', '["That it falls linearly along the line, since the term is applied as the drop times the fraction of the length travelled.", "That it is isothermal, since the Joule-Thomson coefficient is applied at the inlet temperature rather than station by station.", "That it is measured, since the module declines to build a station table out of a pressure drop it was not given both ends of.", "That it is small, since a large drop would move the fluid properties the heat balance was built on."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The module solves no hydraulics, so the shape of the traverse between the two supplied pressures is a decision the module made rather than one it computed.'),
  ('flowassurance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Why is every margin computed in this course conditional, and what should a report say about it?', '["Because the engines are validated only on the published cases, so a margin computed off those conditions carries an unquantified extrapolation with it.", "Because the Joule-Thomson term is applied undamped, so every margin on a gas line is wrong by the damping factor of 1.730568266886.", "Because ok true is not a verdict, so a margin should always be reported with the term count and the reference diameter beside it.", "Because the hydrate boundary is an input from a laboratory or a flash, so a margin should be reported with that number and its source beside it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both module headers say the boundary is a fluid property that comes from whoever owns the fluid, and the engines never draw the line every verdict is measured against.');
